# `rt_binop` used to hand an operand's own handle back for any slot that was
# not a plain value, so `1 + d` ANSWERED `d` and the page printed `<io>` where
# both other engines refused. A silent wrong answer rather than a divergent
# sentence, which is why the three-engine walk was the only thing that could
# have found it.
# The check refuses a provable box here now (the errors corpus pins that
# sentence), so the box travels through a list, where nothing can prove it.
import "std/io"

pub fn opaque v
  (push [] v)[1]!

d = io/write "a\n" >> io/write "b\n"

pub play = print "{1 + opaque d}"
